  "body": "## Finding Description and Impact\n\n`Distributor._setClaimRecipient` stores `claimRecipient[user][token] = recipient` but emits `ClaimRecipientUpdated(user, recipient, token)` while the event is declared as `(user, token, recipient)`. As a result, the log’s indexed `token` topic actually holds the recipient address and the `recipient` topic holds the token address. Off-chain indexers that rebuild `claimRecipient` from events will construct the inverse mapping and surface incorrect recipient/token pairs.\n\nThe mismatch is visible in the event declaration at [`contracts/Distributor.sol:119-120`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2025-11-merkl/blob/main/contracts/Distributor.sol#L119-L120) and the emit site at [`contracts/Distributor.sol:540-546`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2025-11-merkl/blob/main/contracts/Distributor.sol#L540-L546):\n\n```solidity\n// contracts/Distributor.sol Lines 266-274\n\nfunction setClaimRecipient(address recipient, address token) external {\n    _setClaimRecipient(msg.sender, recipient, token);\n}\n\n/// @notice Toggles a main operator's authorization to claim tokens on behalf of any user\n/// @param operator Operator whose status is being toggled\n/// @param token Token for which authorization applies (zero address = all tokens)\n/// @dev Only callable by guardian for an individual token or governor if it's for all tokens\n/// @dev Main operators can claim for any user without individual user authorization\n```\n\n\n```solidity\n// contracts/Distributor.sol Lines 544-549\n\nfunction _setClaimRecipient(address user, address recipient, address token) internal {\n    claimRecipient[user][token] = recipient;\n    emit ClaimRecipientUpdated(user, recipient, token);\n}\n\n/// @notice Ensures the caller has governor role\n\n\n```\n\nBecause many analytics pipelines and payout automation jobs consume events instead of on-chain storage, they will misroute or misreport claim recipients for a given token, leading to phantom, duplicated, or misdirected off-chain payouts and discrepancies between UI displays and true on-chain state.\n\n### Affected code\n- [`contracts/Distributor.sol:119-120`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2025-11-merkl/blob/main/contracts/Distributor.sol#L119-L120) event parameter order `(user, token, recipient)`\n- [`contracts/Distributor.sol:540-546`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2025-11-merkl/blob/main/contracts/Distributor.sol#L540-L546) emit passes `(user, recipient, token)`, swapping topics\n\n---\n## Impact\n\n- Off-chain indexers reconstruct `claimRecipient` incorrectly, pairing tokens with the wrong recipients.\n- Any reporting, accounting, or automated payout system that trusts the event will misroute funds or surface incorrect recipient metadata.\n- Event/state divergence makes on-chain truth and off-chain observability inconsistent, potentially causing user-facing discrepancies and operational errors.\n\n---\n## Recommended mitigation steps\n\n1. Emit `ClaimRecipientUpdated(user, token, recipient)` in `_setClaimRecipient` to align log topics with storage.\n2. Add a regression test that records the event and asserts topic ordering matches the mapping write.\n3. If historical events must be consumed, publish a one-time notice or migration script to rebuild accurate mappings from storage rather than logs.\n\n---",
  "proof_of_concept": "## PoC Test `test_ClaimRecipientUpdated_EventSwapsTokenAndRecipient` that will run in (test/c4/C4PoC.t.sol)\n\n### Exploit Sequence\n1. Deploy a fresh `Distributor` and set Alice’s recipient for `ANGLE` to Bob.\n2. Record the emitted `ClaimRecipientUpdated` log and decode its indexed topics.\n3. Observe that `topics[2]` (token) equals Bob and `topics[3]` (recipient) equals `ANGLE`, proving the swap while storage correctly maps `(alice, ANGLE) -> bob`.\n\n### Test file \n\n```solidity\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: UNLICENSED\n\npragma solidity ^0.8.17;\n\nimport { Fixture } from \"../Fixture.t.sol\";\nimport { console } from \"forge-std/console.sol\";\nimport { Vm } from \"forge-std/Vm.sol\";\nimport { Distributor } from \"../../contracts/Distributor.sol\";\nimport { IAccessControlManager } from \"../../contracts/interfaces/IAccessControlManager.sol\";\n\ncontract C4PoC is Fixture {\n    Distributor public distributor;\n    Distributor public distributorImpl;\n\n    function setUp() public override {\n        super.setUp();\n\n        // Deploy a fresh Distributor proxy hooked to the shared access control manager.\n        distributorImpl = new Distributor();\n        distributor = Distributor(deployUUPS(address(distributorImpl), hex\"\"));\n        distributor.initialize(IAccessControlManager(address(accessControlManager)));\n    }\n\n    function test_ClaimRecipientUpdated_EventSwapsTokenAndRecipient() external {\n        // Phase 1: Alice configures Bob as her recipient for ANGLE claims.\n        address token = address(angle);\n        address recipient = bob;\n        vm.recordLogs();\n        vm.prank(alice);\n        distributor.setClaimRecipient(recipient, token);\n\n        // Phase 2: Pull the emitted ClaimRecipientUpdated log.\n        Vm.Log[] memory entries = vm.getRecordedLogs();\n        assertEq(entries.length, 1, \"expected exactly one ClaimRecipientUpdated log\");\n        Vm.Log memory log = entries[0];\n        bytes32 expectedSig = keccak256(\"ClaimRecipientUpdated(address,address,address)\");\n        assertEq(log.topics[0], expectedSig, \"unexpected event emitted\");\n\n        // Phase 3: Decode indexed topics; bug puts recipient into the token slot and token into the recipient slot.\n        address topicUser = address(uint160(uint256(log.topics[1])));\n        address topicToken = address(uint160(uint256(log.topics[2])));\n        address topicRecipient = address(uint160(uint256(log.topics[3])));\n\n        // Phase 4: Compare on-chain storage with what the event advertised.\n        address storedRecipient = distributor.claimRecipient(alice, token);\n        console.log(\"claimRecipient mapping value\", storedRecipient);\n        console.log(\"event topic user\", topicUser);\n        console.log(\"event topic token (expected token address)\", topicToken);\n        console.log(\"event topic recipient (expected recipient address)\", topicRecipient);\n\n        // Storage is correct (recipient stored under the token key).\n        assertEq(storedRecipient, recipient);\n        assertEq(topicUser, alice, \"user topic should match caller\");\n\n        // The event encodes recipient and token in reversed positions, proving the mismatch.\n        assertEq(topicToken, recipient, \"token topic incorrectly holds the recipient address\");\n        assertEq(topicRecipient, token, \"recipient topic incorrectly holds the token address\");\n    }\n}\n\n\n```\n\nrun \n\n```bash\nforge test --match-path test/c4/C4PoC.t.sol -vv\n```\n\ntest results \n\n```bash\nRan 1 test for test/c4/C4PoC.t.sol:C4PoC\n[PASS] test_ClaimRecipientUpdated_EventSwapsTokenAndRecipient() (gas: 58702)\nLogs:\n  claimRecipient mapping value 0x2B5AD5c4795c026514f8317c7a215E218DcCD6cF\n  event topic user 0x7E5F4552091A69125d5DfCb7b8C2659029395Bdf\n  event topic token (expected token address) 0x2B5AD5c4795c026514f8317c7a215E218DcCD6cF\n  event topic recipient (expected recipient address) 0x5615dEB798BB3E4dFa0139dFa1b3D433Cc23b72f\n\nSuite result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 0 skipped; finished in 4.04ms (363.25µs CPU time)\n```",
